[[Alan Gordon (songwriter)|Alan Gordon]] had served as the drummer and one of the primary songwriters of [[The Magicians (band)|the Magicians]], which had recently evolved from Tex & the Chex.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Unterberger |first=Richie |title=The Magicians {{!}} Biography & History|url=https://www.allmusic.com/artist/the-magicians-mn0000759371/biography|access-date=June 11, 2021|website=AllMusic|language=en}}</ref> He wrote and co-wrote a number of songs for the band, most famously "An Invitation to Cry" (with Jimmy Woods).<ref>Bush, John. {{Cite web|title=An Invitation to Cry - The Magicians {{!}} Song Info |website=[[AllMusic]]|url=https://www.allmusic.com/song/an-invitation-to-cry-mt0008883906|language=en|access-date=June 11, 2021}}</ref> Gordon already had many of the lyrics in mind for "Happy Together" but the chorus of the song came to him at the Park Street Diner in [[Ayer, Massachusetts]], after visiting his father. The [[melody]] was based on the constant tuning of guitarist Allan "Jake" Jacobs during the band's concerts.<ref name=":12">{{Cite AV media|title=The Turtles History - Happy Together Documentary|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_ubb3kHHI70 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211221/_ubb3kHHI70 |archive-date=December 21, 2021 |url-status=live|access-date=June 14, 2021|website=YouTube}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref name=":11" /> After a failed attempt to convince Jacobs to write the song with him, Gordon finished it with vocalist Garry Bonner.<ref name=":11">{{Cite web|last=|title=Happy Together by The Turtles - Songfacts|url=https://www.songfacts.com/facts/the-turtles/happy-together|access-date=June 11, 2021|website=Songfacts.com|language=en}}</ref><ref name=":13" />

In 1965, Bonner and Gordon started to work with publishers [[Charles Koppelman]] and Don Rubin from the Koppelman/Rubin Associates, writing songs for artists selected by them.<ref name=":13">{{Cite web |title=The Alan Gordon Music Library - The MusicAlley part 3 |url=https://www.wearehappytogether.com/themusicalley03.html |access-date=June 12, 2021 |website=Wearehappytogether.com}}</ref> The songwriters cut a demo of "Happy Together" and offered it to several artists including [[the Happenings]], [[the Vogues]] and [[the Tokens]], but it was rejected by all of them.<ref name=":03">{{Cite book|last=Wadhams|first=Wayne|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=UVJDvitqIvIC|title=Inside the Hits|publisher=Berklee Press|year=2001|isbn=978-0-634-01430-7|editor-last=Nathan|editor-first=David|page=254|language=en|editor-last2=Gedutis|editor-first2=Susan}}</ref><ref name=":5">{{Cite web|last=O'Rourke|first=Sally|title=It Was 50 Years Ago Today: The Turtles Elevate "Happy Together" With a Dose of Irony|url=http://www.rebeatmag.com/it-was-50-years-ago-today-the-turtles-elevate-happy-together-with-a-dose-of-irony/|access-date=June 8, 2021|website=Rebeatmag.com}}</ref>

[[File:The Turtles.png|thumb|[[The Turtles]] in 1967. One of the few photos showing [[Chip Douglas]] (pictured in the upper left) with the group.|left]]

Around the same time, [[California]] band [[the Turtles]] had been struggling with financial and personal problems. After scoring three [[Top 40]] hits in 1965 and 1966, including [[Bob Dylan]]'s "[[It Ain't Me Babe]]" and "[[You Baby (song)|You Baby]]", their next five singles had not charted well. [[Chip Douglas]] and [[John Barbata]] had stepped in as replacements for the original members.<ref name=":14" /><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Logan |first1=Nick |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=boo4AQAAIAAJ&q=Happy+Together+the+turtles |title=The Illustrated Encyclopedia of Rock |last2=Woffinden |first2=Bob |date=1978 |publisher=Harmony Books |isbn=978-0-517-53410-6 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Hochman |first=Steve |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=RyAKAQAAMAAJ&q=Happy+Together+the+turtles |title=Popular Musicians: Sonny and Cher-ZZ Top |date=1999 |publisher=Salem Press |isbn=978-0-89356-990-7 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Joynson |first=Vernon |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=tQc5AQAAIAAJ&q=Happy+Together+the+turtles |title=The Acid Trip: A Complete Guide to Psychedelic Music |date=1984 |publisher=Babylon Books |isbn=978-0-907188-24-7|language=en}}</ref> As an act focused on singles, member [[Mark Volman]] explained that their lack of success almost caused the Turtles to break up.<ref name=":14">{{Cite book |last=Voger |first=Mark |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2Y8-DwAAQBAJ&q=happy+together+the+turtles&pg=PA34 |title=Groovy: When Flower Power Bloomed in Pop Culture |date=November 15, 2017 |publisher=TwoMorrows Publishing |isbn=978-1-60549-080-9 |pages=34 |language=en}}</ref>{{Sfn|Kaylan|Tamarkin|2013}}

Lead vocalist [[Howard Kaylan]] mentions in his autobiography, and in the Turtles' documentary, that the group found "Happy Together" after spending some time to listen to demos submitted by publishing companies.<ref name=":9" /><ref name=":12" />{{Refn|According to Alan Gordon's website, Koppelman/Rubin arranged for their West Coast associate, [[Gary Klein (producer)|Gary Klein]], to submit the demo for the Turtles' label, complementing Kaylan's version of the "publishing companies" [[White Whale Records|White Whale]].<ref name=":13" />|group=nb}} Volman remembers it slightly differently, saying that, between the sets of a [[Manhattan]] gig, Bonner and Gordon themselves asked the band if they needed material, and then subsequently sent some demos that included "Happy Together".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pinnock |first=Bruce |date=April 22, 2019 |title=The Turtles on 'Happy Together': "Everybody needs a big hit" {{!}} Page 2 of 5 |url=https://www.uncut.co.uk/features/turtles-happy-together-everybody-needs-big-hit-110324/ |access-date=June 14, 2021 |website=UNCUT |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref name=":7">{{Cite web |date=September 1, 2007 |title=Classic Tracks: The Turtles' "Happy Together" |url=https://www.mixonline.com/recording/classic-tracks-turtles-happy-together-365864 |access-date=June 9, 2021 |website=Mixonline |language=en-US}}</ref> Because of its circulation to many other artists, the demo [[acetate disc|acetate]] was worn out and, according to [[Fred Bronson]], "practically unlistenable", with Kaylan describing the acetate as "scratchy and sticky".<ref name=":03" /><ref name=":10">{{Cite book |last=Bronson |first=Fred |url=http://archive.org/details/billboardbookofnbro00bron |title=The Billboard Book of Number One Hits |publisher=Billboard Publications |others=Internet Archive |year=1988 |isbn=978-0-8230-7545-4 |location=New York |pages=221}}</ref>{{Sfn|Kaylan|Tamarkin|2013}}

During an interview with [[Grammy Awards]], Howard Kaylan said that, when he heard it the first time, he considered the recording to be "terrible", but later understood that the rejection by other artists was due to the performance.<ref name=":15">{{Cite web|title=Howard Kaylan - Story Behind "Happy Together" |via=[[YouTube]]| date=July 2, 2013 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hrPzSEnXqzA |language=en |access-date=June 12, 2021}}</ref> Kaylan recalled that the demo consisted of "one guy (Gary Bonner) strumming an acoustic guitar while the other (Alan Gordon) sings in a bizarre [[falsetto]] to get a semblance of rhythm going. Their voices were abysmal".<ref name=":9">{{Cite book|last1=Kaylan|first1=Howard|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=SQ5OjILuPywC&q=shell+shocked+howard+kaylan|title=Shell Shocked: My Life with the Turtles, Flo and Eddie, and Frank Zappa, etc.|last2=Tamarkin|first2=Jeff|date=March 1, 2013|publisher=Backbeat Books|isbn=978-1-4803-4293-4|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Jerry Reuss - The Turtles - Happy Together|url=https://www.jerryreuss.com/theturtleshappytogether.html|access-date=June 12, 2021|website=Jerryreuss.com}}</ref> Volman agreed with Kaylan, calling the demo "amateurish, lacking any kind of professional performance".<ref name=":7" />

Conversely, [[Brill Building]] [[session musician|session guitarist]] Ralph Casale, in an interview with [[Songfacts]] affirmed that he had played on the demo and that other session musicians were involved. The performance was in a "[[The Lovin' Spoonful|Lovin' Spoonful]] feel" and praised it as much like a "finished product".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Songfacts |title=Ralph Casale - Session Pro : Songwriter Interviews |url=https://www.songfacts.com/blog/interviews/ralph-casale-session-pro |access-date=June 12, 2021 |website=Songfacts.com |language=en}}</ref>{{Refn|Casale's recollection about the demo is possibly mistaken, according to Bonner and Gordon's supposed performance of the demo in the Turtles' documentary,<ref name=":12"/> also the Kaylan/Volman's version is the most cited.<ref name=":9" /><ref name=":5" />|group=nb}}

