The sheet music for "Happy Together" is notated in the common key of [[E minor]],<ref name=":8">{{Cite web |url=https://www.musicnotes.com/sheetmusic/mtd.asp?ppn=MN0068549#arrangement |title=The Turtles "Happy Together" Sheet Music in E Minor |website=Musicnotes.com |date=October 6, 2008 |access-date=November 3, 2018 }}</ref> but the Turtles' recording is one whole step higher, in [[F-sharp minor|F{{sharp}} minor]] during the verses and [[F-sharp major|F{{sharp}} major]] (F{{sharp}} Mixolydian<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.hooktheory.com/theorytab/view/the-turtles/happy-together|title=Happy Together by The Turtles Chords, Melody, and Music Theory Analysis |website=Hooktheory.com}}</ref>) during the chorus.{{Sfn|Wadhams|2001|p=253}}<ref name=":6">{{Cite web|last=Langerholc|first=Emily|title=Changing Moods & Modes: Minor to Major Modulation (and Picardy Third) in The Turtles' "Happy Together"|url=http://www.rebelmusicteacher.com/blog/2016/4/8/minor-to-major-modes-in-the-turtles-happy-together|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190425091431/http://www.rebelmusicteacher.com/blog/2016/4/8/minor-to-major-modes-in-the-turtles-happy-together|url-status=usurped|archive-date=April 25, 2019|access-date=December 5, 2020|website=Rebelmusicteacher.com|date=April 28, 2016 }}</ref> The song is in [[Time signature#Characteristics|common time]] with a [[tempo]] of 120 beats per minute.<ref>{{cite web|title=The Turtles - Happy Together bpm|url=https://songbpm.com/@the-turtles/happy-together|access-date=November 3, 2018|website=Songbpm.com}}</ref> It starts with an electric guitar, followed by drums and electric bass, then Kaylan starts to sing the first two verses, reinforced by another guitar and [[reverberation|reverberated]] vocal harmonies in the second verse,<ref name=":03" /> before the chorus begins (consisting of [[Double tracking|double-tracked]] lead vocals now backed by four part "ah" singing and trumpets).<ref name=":5" /><ref name=":03" />{{Sfn|Wadhams|2001|p=255}}

The switch of mode from minor key verse to major key chorus is called by Langerholc "one of the most effective minor to major switcharoos of all time". The pivot chord for the change of mode is C{{sharp}} (V), the dominant of [[Modulation (music)|both]]<ref name=":6" /> F{{sharp}} minor and major.{{Sfn|Wadhams|2001|p=253}} This structure accompanies Kaylan in the third verse, the second chorus, the first repeat of the third verse (harmonizing with Volman), the "baa, baa" chorus and the [[conclusion (music)|outro]]: the second repeat of the third with the [[Coda (music)|coda]], that consists of another "baa, baa" section with the joining of the orchestra and the band, before the reverb fades the song.{{Sfn|Wadhams|2001|p=256}}{{Sfn|Wadhams|2001|p=255}}<ref name=":5" /><ref name=":8" /> The outro (F{{sharp}} minor verse with the F{{sharp}} major coda) is a [[Picardy third]].<ref name=":6" />{{Refn|1=A [[Mellotron]] and an organ are used in the third verse and its second repeat, as well as in the chorus,{{Sfn|Wadhams|2001|pp=255–256}} while [[oboes]] (or organs mimicking them) are heard in the first repeat.<ref>{{Cite web|title=13 unbelievable oboe solos you didn't even notice in pop music|url=https://www.classicfm.com/discover-music/instruments/oboe/oboe-solos-in-pop-music/|access-date=June 17, 2021|website=Classic FM|language=en}}</ref>{{Sfn|Wadhams|2001|p=256}}|group=nb}}

Many listeners (such as Langerholc<ref name=":6" />) thought that the lyrics of "Happy Together" were about a couple in love with each other due to its cheery chorus and the title, but, according to historian James E. Perone, a closer reading in the lyrics ("imagine me and you", "if I should call you") reveals that the love expressed by the narrator is not reciprocated by the other person, with Perone stating that the relationship "is only in dreams, wishes, desires and the mind of the singer".<ref>{{Cite book |last=Perone |first=James E. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=68xwDwAAQBAJ&q=Happy+Together+the+turtles&pg=PA92 |title=Listen to Pop! Exploring a Musical Genre |date=September 1, 2018 |publisher=ABC-CLIO |isbn=978-1-4408-6377-6 |pages=93 |language=en}}</ref> O'Rourke felt that the song "stretches the contrast between the loneliness of being apart and the thrill of being together to bipolar extremes".<ref name=":5" /> Joe Viglione compared "Happy Together"'s lyrics to [[Tommy James and the Shondells|Tommy James]]'s hit "[[Mirage (Tommy James and the Shondells song)|Mirage]]", but while "Mirage" was about an ex-girlfriend, "the Turtles never even get to first base in their laments".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Viglione |first=Joe |title="Mirage" review |url=https://www.allmusic.com/song/mirage-mt0001415891 |access-date=June 17, 2021 |website=AllMusic}}</ref> O'Rourke observed that is the listener's option to determine if in the final line "We're happy together", the narrator conquered his beloved, "or if he's just retreated into his fantasy world for good".<ref name=":5" />

